// `staff` — read staff notation, and produce a `std::notation::staff` document.
//
// A region of this adapter is a page of staff notation written in text. It
// expands to one ordinary expression — a call to the staff package's
// `Document` constructor — resolved, checked, and evaluated exactly as if the
// composer had written it out. The adapter reads; the package decides. Nothing
// here computes a sounding pitch, a performed duration, an exact tuplet span,
// or a grace note's timing: an adapter that pre-computed them would be
// deciding at read time what the package defers.
//
// # The spelling
//
//     instrument "bb_clarinet"  transposing M2  clef treble
//     key d major  time (4, 4)  spelling shortest_readable
//
//     pickup (1, 4) { g4/4 }
//     repeat (2) {
//         bar (4, 4) { c5/4 d5/4. e5/8 f5/4 ~ }
//         bar (4, 4) { f5/8 rest/8 [g4 b4 d5]/4 c5(3/8) rest/8 }
//         ending (1) { bar (4, 4) { slur { tuplet (3, 2) { a4/8 b4/8 c5/8 } } } }
//         ending (2) { meter (3, 4) bar (3, 4) { g5/4 a5/4 b5/4 } }
//     }
//
// One rule covers every form in it: a **word**, then its **parentheses** if it
// takes numbers, then its **braces** if it holds notation. `bar (4, 4) { … }`,
// `tuplet (3, 2) { … }` and `repeat (2) { … }` are the same shape, and
// `slur { … }` is that shape with the numbers left off. A chord and a grace
// hold pitches rather than notation, so they take brackets instead of braces:
// `[g4 b4 d5]/4` and `grace [e5]`.
//
// That rule is `knows` below, and it is the whole dispatch table: a word this
// staff knows either **nests** notation, **sounds** one event, or **states**
// one of the six facts a document carries in its head.
//
// # How it is read
//
// `recurse_syntax` enters a node before its children and hands each child the
// context the parent chose for it, so this adapter is one traversal. Three
// places are the whole of the inherited context — a body, a form's
// parentheses, a chord's brackets — and they decide the one question a token
// cannot answer about itself. A body is read **forwards**: a word before its
// parentheses and its braces, a pitch before the value written on it, a tie
// before whatever it reaches. What is being read is a `Pending`, finished by
// whatever starts the next thing — `c5/4.` is a note the moment `f5` or `bar`
// or the end of the body arrives, and until then it is still taking dots.
// That is what prompt 141's `Cons` bought: the data this adapter emits is
// right-nested, so a reader that emitted as it went had to run from the end.
// This one **stacks** what it has placed, newest first, and turns the stack
// into that expression once, in `sequenced` — and a tie is checked against the
// note it actually reaches rather than against the note in hand.
//
// # Why it is not the layout a musician would draw
//
// The compiler hands an adapter the fixed lexer's tokens grouped by matched
// delimiters and nothing else. There are no lines — `Whitespace` is one
// undifferentiated trivia kind — so the forms are delimited instead, and
// `time` opens a staff where `meter` changes one because a word written above
// the music and the same word written among it arrive here identically. A time
// signature is written either way — `time (4, 4)` and `time 4/4` are the same
// two numbers — because a rational reaches this adapter in the parts the lexer
// found, so the count and the unit are the numerals the composer typed and not
// the one value `4/4` and `1/1` both reduce to. A signature is a count of a
// unit rather than a quotient, and both spellings say which count and which
// unit. `bar (4, 4) { … }` says its own meter for the reason a note states its own
// octave: whether one bar is full can then be answered without looking
// anywhere else, which is what lets the check land on the bar the composer
// wrote. `pickup (1, 4) { … }` is a bar of a stated length that is not the
// meter, and it produces an ordinary `Bar`.
//
// # What this adapter is written with
//
// - **Every construction is a quote**, so a literal node's place is derived
//   from the quote it stands in: no call-assembling helper, no role integer,
//   and no identity for anybody to keep unique.
// - **The composer's own token is spliced, not rebuilt** — through
//   `as_expression`, which runs the real parser over the node handed over, so
//   Origin, `edit`, and `print` reach back to what was typed. `key_named` is
//   the one exception and says why in its own note.
// - **A tie's continuation is checked by spelling, not by pitch**, there being
//   no operation that reads a pitch out of a token: `f5` tied to `f#5` is
//   caught by comparing the two spellings the composer wrote. That is a whole
//   comparison and not a coarse one — the parts partition the lexeme, so two
//   equal spellings are a letter, an accidental and an octave that agree.
// - **A printed page is a new page.** `print` carries nothing across — no
//   comment, no blank line, no origin — because the value holds none of those.
//   Preserving them is `edit`'s job, which is why the two are separate.
// - **A refusal does not quote the numbers it computed, and could now.** "This
//   bar does not hold what it says it measures" points at the bar and stops
//   there; `ratio_literal` would say by how much. That is prompt 127dd's.
library {
    // `range` is ordinary source now that `Iterable` carries the folds, so the
    // printer that uses it says where it comes from. An adapter may not import
    // a *syntax* — that is the bootstrap cycle — but an ordinary import only
    // brings declarations in.
    import std::list;

    // The highest of the three levels, and a promise the compiler checks where
    // this module is imported: this adapter reads a region, serves one
    // structured command against one, and writes a whole page out of a value.
    let level = "generative";

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- reading

    // Why a region was refused, and the node the refusal is about.
    //
    // A refusal carries a node rather than a place: the compiler reads the
    // range off the node, and an adapter has no operation for reading one.
    data Refusal {
        NoRefusal,
        Refused(node: Syntax<TokenTree>, why: Text),
    }

    // The first refusal of two, in the order they were written.
    fn first_refusal(found: Refusal, later: Refusal) -> Refusal {
        match found {
            Refused(node, why) -> found,
            NoRefusal -> later,
        }
    }

    // What one item sounds, by the spelling the composer wrote for it.
    //
    // A tie reaches whatever sounds next. Read forwards, "next" is whatever
    // arrives after the tie, so a hanging tie is carried along until an item
    // that sounds meets it — and a rest is `Silent`, so it does not.
    data Sound {
        Silent,
        Voice(spelling: Text, node: Syntax<TokenTree>),
    }

    // A tie waiting for what it reaches. It is threaded into every body a form
    // holds, so a tie written across a bar line is met inside the next bar.
    data Hanging {
        Closed,
        Hangs(spelling: Text, node: Syntax<TokenTree>),
    }

    // One header field, once it has been read.
    data Given {
        Absent,
        Stated(given: Syntax<Expr>),
    }

    // The six things a staff says about itself before any notation.
    record Head {
        instrument: Given;
        shift: Given;
        written_clef: Given;
        tonality: Given;
        beats: Given;
        spelled: Given;
    }

    // What a form is, once the numbers it stated have been read.
    //
    // The exact rational each one carries is what it does to written time:
    // a bar measures what it says, a tuplet scales what it holds, and the
    // other three take their body's own span.
    data Form {
        Barred(beats: Syntax<Expr>, measures: Ratio),
        Slurred,
        Grouped(played: Syntax<Expr>, against: Syntax<Expr>, factor: Ratio),
        Looped(times: Syntax<Expr>),
        Volta(pass: Syntax<Expr>),
    }

    // One item, placed. Notation is read forwards and stacked newest first,
    // and `sequenced` turns the stack into a right-nested `StaffItem` once.
    data Placed {
        Sang(spot: NodePath, event: Syntax<Expr>),
        Nested(spot: NodePath, form: Form, body: Syntax<Expr>),
    }

    // What one stretch of notation reads to.
    record Reading {
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>;
        items: List<Placed>;
        span: Ratio;
        hangs: Hanging;
        head: Head;
        refusal: Refusal;
    }

    // One more dot, or nothing further.
    //
    // The count is a `Nat`. It used to be a four-value `data Dots`, and the
    // declaration said why: the count has to be emitted as a numeral, and this
    // adapter could only emit numerals it could spell. `syntax_numeral` spells
    // any of them, so the enumeration went and the number stayed.
    //
    // Three is still the most a note takes, and that is this adapter's
    // reading of the notation rather than a limit of what it can write: a
    // fourth dot is refused with a sentence, in `dot_read`.
    fn one_more_dot(sofar: Nat) -> Option<Nat> {
        match sofar {
            3 -> None,
            _ -> Some(nat_add(sofar, 1)),
        }
    }

    // What the dots multiply the undotted value by: each dot adds half of what
    // the dot before it added. The last arm is the third dot rather than a
    // default, because `one_more_dot` above is the only way a count grows.
    fn dotted_factor(dots: Nat) -> Ratio {
        match dots {
            0 -> 1/1,
            1 -> 3/2,
            2 -> 7/4,
            _ -> 15/8,
        }
    }

    // The written value a note or rest states, waiting for what it belongs to.
    //
    // `Divided` carries the numeral's own rational rather than the span it
    // works out to, because reading forwards the dots arrive after the value
    // and each one changes that span. The value is never zero: the reader
    // refuses `c5/0` where it is written.
    data Length {
        NoLength,
        Divided(numeral: Syntax<Expr>, dots: Nat, value: Ratio),
        Spanned(numeral: Syntax<Expr>, span: Ratio),
    }

    // Whether what is being read is tied on to whatever sounds next.
    data Tying {
        Untied,
        Tied(node: Syntax<TokenTree>),
    }

    // Up to two numbers a form stated in its parentheses.
    data Numbers {
        NoNumbers,
        OneNumber(first: Syntax<Expr>, value: Ratio),
        TwoNumbers(first: Syntax<Expr>, count: Ratio, second: Syntax<Expr>, unit: Ratio),
    }

    // A braces body that has been read but not yet used.
    data Taken {
        NoBody,
        Took(spot: NodePath, read: Reading),
    }

    // One word or literal, as the lexer handed it over.
    record Said {
        spot: NodePath;
        kind: TokenKind;
        spelling: Text;
    }

    // The pitches of a chord or a grace group, waiting for what holds them.
    data Voiced {
        NoVoices,
        NoPitches(spot: NodePath),
        Voices(spot: NodePath, spelling: Text, listed: Syntax<Expr>),
    }

    // The words a header field took after it. `key d major` is the only form
    // in this notation that takes two.
    data Words {
        NoWords,
        OneWord(only: Said),
        TwoWords(first: Said, second: Said),
    }

    // The one word a field took, if it took exactly one.
    fn only_word(words: Words) -> Option<Said> {
        match words {
            NoWords -> None,
            OneWord(only) -> Some(only),
            TwoWords(first, second) -> None,
        }
    }

    // One more of them.
    fn one_more_word(words: Words, word: Said) -> Words {
        match words {
            NoWords -> OneWord(word),
            OneWord(only) -> TwoWords(only, word),
            TwoWords(first, second) -> words,
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------- the words

    // Which of the five forms a nesting word opens, which of the three items
    // a sounding word writes, and which of the six things the head says a word
    // states. Each case carries the word it was opened with, for the reason
    // `Knows` below gives.
    data Nesting {
        AsBar(word: Text),
        AsSlur(word: Text),
        AsTuplet(word: Text),
        AsRepeat(word: Text),
        AsEnding(word: Text),
    }

    data Sounding {
        AsRest(word: Text),
        AsGrace(word: Text),
        AsMeter(word: Text),
    }

    data Says {
        AsInstrument(word: Text),
        AsTransposing(word: Text),
        AsClef(word: Text),
        AsSpelling(word: Text),
        AsKey(word: Text),
        AsTime(word: Text),
    }

    // What this staff knows a word to be.
    //
    // One table, and the whole dispatch: a word either **nests** notation,
    // **sounds** one item, or **states** one of the six facts a document
    // carries in its head. Anything else is a word this staff does not know,
    // and is held rather than refused here, because a word this staff *does*
    // know is often what says the held one was an argument. Every case carries
    // what the reading after it needs, which is not decoration: an arm that
    // takes no argument is evaluated whichever arm is chosen.
    data Knows {
        Unknown(said: Said),
        Nests(said: Said, form: Nesting, missing: Text),
        Sings(said: Said, sings: Sounding, missing: Text),
        States(said: Said, field: Says),
    }

    fn knows(said: Said) -> Knows {
        match said.spelling {
            "rest" -> Sings(said, AsRest("rest"), "this rest states no written value"),
            "bar" -> Nests(said, AsBar("bar"), "this bar holds no notation"),
            "pickup" -> Nests(said, AsBar("pickup"), "this bar holds no notation"),
            "slur" -> Nests(said, AsSlur("slur"), "this slur holds no notation"),
            "tuplet" -> Nests(said, AsTuplet("tuplet"), "this tuplet holds no notation"),
            "grace" -> Sings(said, AsGrace("grace"), "this grace holds no pitches"),
            "repeat" -> Nests(
                said,
                AsRepeat("repeat"),
                "this repeat does not say how many times it holds what",
            ),
            "ending" -> Nests(
                said,
                AsEnding("ending"),
                "this ending does not say which pass it holds what for",
            ),
            "meter" -> Sings(said, AsMeter("meter"), "this meter states no count and unit"),
            "time" -> States(said, AsTime("time")),
            "instrument" -> States(said, AsInstrument("instrument")),
            "transposing" -> States(said, AsTransposing("transposing")),
            "clef" -> States(said, AsClef("clef")),
            "spelling" -> States(said, AsSpelling("spelling")),
            "key" -> States(said, AsKey("key")),
            _ -> Unknown(said),
        }
    }

    // The word itself, whichever of the four this staff made of it.
    fn said_of(known: Knows) -> Said {
        match known {
            Unknown(word) | Nests(word, _, _) | Sings(word, _, _) | States(word, _) -> word,
        }
    }

    // What began the thing being read: a pitch, a word, or nothing yet.
    data Started {
        Nothing,
        Sung(said: Said),
        Worded(known: Knows),
    }

    // What is in hand, waiting for whatever finishes it.
    //
    // Reading forwards, a thing is finished by whatever starts the next one:
    // `c5/4.` is a note the moment `f5` or `bar` or the end of the body
    // arrives, and until then it is still taking dots.
    record Pending {
        started: Started;
        length: Length;
        tying: Tying;
        numbers: Numbers;
        taken: Taken;
        voiced: Voiced;
        words: Words;
    }

    // Where the reader is, which is the one thing a token cannot say about
    // itself — and each place carries exactly what it can hold. `4` in a body
    // is a written value, `4` between a form's parentheses is one of the
    // numbers that form states, and `g4` between a chord's brackets is one of
    // its pitches. Between parentheses there is no sequence to place into and
    // no head to state, and between brackets there is one token and nothing
    // else, so neither of those two carries a `Reading` at all.
    //
    // The fourth is inside one lexeme rather than inside the music: a fused
    // group's children are the parts of a single `c#5` or `3/8`, and reading
    // them is reading a token, not reading a place.
    data State {
        Body(read: Reading, held: Pending),
        Parens(numbers: Numbers, length: Length),
        Bracket(said: Option<Said>),
        Fusing(parts: Parts),
    }

    // The parts of one lexeme, as far as they have been read.
    //
    // `c#5` reaches this adapter as a letter, an accidental and an octave, and
    // `3/8` as a numerator, a bar and a denominator, because that is what the
    // lexer's own pattern found on the way past. This is what they are folded
    // into: the kind they say the lexeme is, the text they spell, and the
    // first and the last of them.
    //
    // Keeping those two is the whole of what parts buy here. A rational read
    // whole is one reduced value, so `4/4` and `1/1` arrive identical; read in
    // parts it is two numerals that are two numbers. Two is enough because no
    // lexeme this staff reads apart has a third.
    record Parts {
        kind: TokenKind;
        spelling: Text;
        opened: Option<NodePath>;
        closed: Option<NodePath>;
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------- emitting

    // Nothing said yet.
    let no_head: Head = Head {
        instrument = Absent,
        shift = Absent,
        written_clef = Absent,
        tonality = Absent,
        beats = Absent,
        spelled = Absent,
    };

    // Nothing read at all, and nothing in hand.
    //
    // Constants rather than functions of a place, which is what building the
    // sequence out of a `List` bought: the empty stack needs no node.
    fn nothing_read(region: Syntax<TokenTree>) -> Reading {
        Reading {
            region = region,
            items = [],
            span = 0/1,
            hangs = Closed,
            head = no_head,
            refusal = NoRefusal,
        }
    }

    let nothing_held: Pending = Pending {
        started = Nothing,
        length = NoLength,
        tying = Untied,
        numbers = NoNumbers,
        taken = NoBody,
        voiced = NoVoices,
        words = NoWords,
    };

    // A rational that is never zero, so that every division below is total.
    // Dividing by zero is a refusal the *checker* makes, and a refused adapter
    // says nothing about the note the author wrote — and guarding the division
    // with a `match` is not enough on its own, because the arm that was not
    // taken has been evaluated too. The reader still refuses the zero where it
    // is written; this keeps the arithmetic from refusing it first.
    fn nonzero(value: Ratio) -> Ratio {
        match ratio_equal(value, 0/1) {
            true -> 1/1,
            false -> value,
        }
    }

    // The node the region holds at a path, for a refusal to point at. The
    // region stands in where there is nothing there, which cannot happen for a
    // path the traversal handed out.
    fn node_at(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, spot: NodePath) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
        syntax_at(region, spot).fold_from_end(
            region,
            fn (node: Syntax<TokenTree>, otherwise: Syntax<TokenTree>) -> Syntax<TokenTree> {
                node
            },
        )
    }

    // The composer's own node at a path, read by the ordinary parser. This is
    // what splices the token the composer wrote rather than rebuilding one
    // from its spelling, so a note points at the `c5` on the page.
    fn expression_at(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, spot: NodePath) -> Option<Syntax<Expr>> {
        match syntax_at(region, spot) {
            None -> None,
            Some(node) -> as_expression(node),
        }
    }

    // Where the compiler has no range for a node.
    fn no_anchor(here: NodePath) -> Syntax<Expr> { quote at here { 0 } }

    // The anchor of the node at `here`. An anchor is a number whose meaning is
    // the compiler's table: this adapter hands one over and can read nothing
    // from it. One call per node is the operation's own law, and the one call
    // is in `emitted` below.
    fn anchored(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, here: NodePath) -> Syntax<Expr> {
        match syntax_anchor(region, here) {
            None -> no_anchor(here),
            Some(node) -> match as_expression(node) {
                None -> no_anchor(here),
                Some(place) -> place,
            },
        }
    }

    // `Tie`, as the package spells it.
    fn tie_of(here: NodePath, tying: Tying) -> Syntax<Expr> {
        match tying {
            Untied -> quote at here {
                Untied
            },
            Tied(node) -> quote at here {
                TiedOn
            },
        }
    }

    // How many dots the emitted value carries, as the numeral it is written
    // with.
    //
    // This was four quotes holding `0`, `1`, `2` and `3` — one per value the
    // count could take — because a computed number had no way into a quote.
    fn dot_count(here: NodePath, dots: Nat) -> Syntax<Expr> { syntax_numeral(here, dots) }

    // `WrittenDuration`, as the package spells it. Every caller has already
    // refused `NoLength` against the note it belongs to, so the whole note
    // below stands for nothing a reader can reach.
    fn value_of(here: NodePath, length: Length) -> Syntax<Expr> {
        match length {
            NoLength -> quote at here {
                NoteValue(1, 0)
            },
            Divided(numeral, dots, value) -> quote at here {
                NoteValue($numeral, ${ dot_count(here, dots) })
            },
            Spanned(numeral, span) -> quote at here {
                ExactSpan($numeral)
            },
        }
    }

    // The exact span a stated written value covers.
    fn stated_span(length: Length) -> Option<Ratio> {
        match length {
            NoLength -> None,
            Divided(numeral, dots, value) -> Some(ratio_div(dotted_factor(dots), value)),
            Spanned(numeral, span) -> Some(span),
        }
    }

    // `Beats(count, unit)`, from the two numbers a form stated.
    fn beats_of(here: NodePath, numbers: Numbers) -> Option<Syntax<Expr>> {
        match numbers {
            TwoNumbers(first, count, second, unit) -> Some(quote at here {
                Beats($first, $second)
            }),
            _ -> None,
        }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------------- placing

    // One item of a written sequence, in front of what follows it.
    fn nested_item(
        spot: NodePath,
        anchor: Syntax<Expr>,
        form: Form,
        body: Syntax<Expr>,
        after: Syntax<Expr>,
    ) -> Syntax<Expr> {
        match form {
            Barred(beats, measures) -> quote at spot {
                Bar($anchor, $beats, $body, $after)
            },
            Slurred -> quote at spot {
                Slur($anchor, $body, $after)
            },
            Grouped(played, against, factor) -> quote at spot {
                Tuplet($anchor, $played, $against, $body, $after)
            },
            Looped(times) -> quote at spot {
                Repeat($anchor, $times, $body, $after)
            },
            Volta(pass) -> quote at spot {
                Ending($anchor, $pass, $body, $after)
            },
        }
    }

    // The one place `syntax_anchor` is called, so that one node is anchored
    // once — the operation's own law rather than a discipline kept by hand.
    fn emitted(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, item: Placed, after: Syntax<Expr>) -> Syntax<Expr> {
        match item {
            Sang(spot, event) -> quote at spot {
                Sounded(${ anchored(region, spot) }, $event, $after)
            },
            Nested(spot, form, body) -> nested_item(
                spot,
                anchored(region, spot),
                form,
                body,
                after,
            ),
        }
    }

    // The stack of placed items, as the right-nested sequence the package
    // holds — newest first, so folding from its start hands each item what
    // follows it.
    fn sequenced(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        items: List<Placed>,
    ) -> Syntax<Expr> {
        items.fold_from_start(
            quote at here {
                NoItems
            },
            fn (after: Syntax<Expr>, item: Placed) -> Syntax<Expr> {
                emitted(region, item, after)
            },
        )
    }

    // A tie left open by what came before, met against what sounds now. It is
    // checked by spelling, and a spelling decides it: the parts of a pitch
    // partition its text, so `f5` and `f#5` being two texts *is* their
    // accidentals differing, which is the mistake worth catching. Reading the
    // parts would buy a sentence naming which part differs, not a verdict.
    fn tie_met(hangs: Hanging, next: Sound) -> Result<Hanging, Refusal> {
        match hangs {
            Closed -> Ok(Closed),
            Hangs(spelling, node) -> match next {
                Silent -> Ok(hangs),
                Voice(other, at_node) -> if text_equal(spelling, other) {
                    Ok(Closed)
                } else {
                    Err(Refused(at_node, "this staff ties a note to a differently spelled one"))
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // The tie left open after an item: its own, where it wrote one, and
    // otherwise whatever was still open once it had been met.
    fn hanging_after(met: Hanging, ties: Hanging) -> Hanging {
        match ties {
            Closed -> met,
            Hangs(spelling, node) -> ties,
        }
    }

    // A tie a note or a chord leaves open.
    fn hanging_of(spelling: Text, tying: Tying) -> Hanging {
        match tying {
            Untied -> Closed,
            Tied(node) -> Hangs(spelling, node),
        }
    }

    // A refusal, with nothing placed.
    fn refusing(read: Reading, spot: NodePath, why: Text) -> Reading {
        read with {
            refusal = first_refusal(read.refusal, Refused(node_at(read.region, spot), why))
        }
    }

    // One sounding item — a note, a rest, a chord, a grace group, a meter
    // change — placed after everything read before it.
    fn sounding(
        read: Reading,
        spot: NodePath,
        event: Syntax<Expr>,
        added: Ratio,
        sounds: Sound,
        ties: Hanging,
    ) -> Reading {
        match tie_met(read.hangs, sounds) {
            Ok(met) -> read with {
                items = Cons(Sang(spot, event), read.items),
                span = ratio_add(read.span, added),
                hangs = hanging_after(met, ties)
            },
            Err(why) -> read with {
                span = ratio_add(read.span, added),
                refusal = first_refusal(read.refusal, why)
            },
        }
    }

    // What a form does to the written time its body covers.
    fn form_span(form: Form, inner: Ratio) -> Ratio {
        match form {
            Barred(beats, measures) -> measures,
            Slurred -> inner,
            Grouped(played, against, factor) -> ratio_mul(inner, factor),
            Looped(times) -> inner,
            Volta(pass) -> inner,
        }
    }

    // A bar states its own length and its contents are checked against it. It
    // lands on the bar the composer wrote because the adapter is the one
    // holding a node: the package could find the fault and not point at it.
    fn form_refusal(node: Syntax<TokenTree>, form: Form, inner: Ratio) -> Refusal {
        match form {
            Barred(beats, measures) -> if ratio_equal(inner, measures) {
                NoRefusal
            } else { Refused(node, "this bar does not hold what it says it measures") },
            Slurred -> NoRefusal,
            Grouped(played, against, factor) -> NoRefusal,
            Looped(times) -> NoRefusal,
            Volta(pass) -> NoRefusal,
        }
    }

    // A nested form, placed with the body it holds. What went wrong inside is
    // reported before what went wrong around it: a bar whose notation the
    // reader could not read does not measure what it says either, and that
    // second sentence is a consequence of the first rather than a second
    // fault. What is open now is what the body left open.
    fn nesting(read: Reading, spot: NodePath, form: Form, body: Reading) -> Reading {
        read with {
            items = Cons(
                Nested(spot, form, sequenced(read.region, spot, body.items)),
                read.items,
            ),
            span = ratio_add(read.span, form_span(form, body.span)),
            hangs = body.hangs,
            refusal = first_refusal(
                read.refusal,
                first_refusal(
                    body.refusal,
                    form_refusal(node_at(read.region, spot), form, body.span),
                ),
            )
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------- the forms

    // What a clef word names.
    fn clef_named(here: NodePath, spelling: Text) -> Option<Syntax<Expr>> {
        match spelling {
            "treble" -> Some(quote at here {
                Treble
            }),
            "bass" -> Some(quote at here {
                Bass
            }),
            "alto" -> Some(quote at here {
                Alto
            }),
            "tenor" -> Some(quote at here {
                Tenor
            }),
            _ -> None,
        }
    }

    // What a spelling word names.
    fn spelling_named(here: NodePath, spelling: Text) -> Option<Syntax<Expr>> {
        match spelling {
            "shortest_readable" -> Some(quote at here {
                ShortestReadable
            }),
            "exact_values" -> Some(quote at here {
                ExactValues
            }),
            _ -> None,
        }
    }

    // `rest/8` — a written silence.
    fn rest_read(read: Reading, spot: NodePath, length: Length, missing: Text) -> Reading {
        match stated_span(length) {
            None -> refusing(read, spot, missing),
            Some(span) -> sounding(
                read,
                spot,
                quote at spot {
                    Rest(${ value_of(spot, length) })
                },
                span,
                Silent,
                Closed,
            ),
        }
    }

    // `c5/4`, `d5/4.`, `f5/4 ~`, `c5(3/8)` — one written note.
    fn note_read(
        read: Reading,
        spot: NodePath,
        spelling: Text,
        length: Length,
        tying: Tying,
    ) -> Reading {
        match stated_span(length) {
            None -> refusing(read, spot, "this note states no written value"),
            Some(span) -> match expression_at(read.region, spot) {
                None -> refusing(read, spot, "this staff cannot read this"),
                Some(sung) -> sounding(
                    read,
                    spot,
                    quote at spot {
                        Note($sung, ${ value_of(spot, length) }, ${ tie_of(spot, tying) })
                    },
                    span,
                    Voice(spelling, node_at(read.region, spot)),
                    hanging_of(spelling, tying),
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // `[g4 b4 d5]/4` — one written chord.
    fn chord_read(
        read: Reading,
        spot: NodePath,
        spelling: Text,
        listed: Syntax<Expr>,
        length: Length,
        tying: Tying,
    ) -> Reading {
        match stated_span(length) {
            None -> refusing(read, spot, "this chord states no written value"),
            Some(span) -> sounding(
                read,
                spot,
                quote at spot {
                    Chord($listed, ${ value_of(spot, length) }, ${ tie_of(spot, tying) })
                },
                span,
                Voice(spelling, node_at(read.region, spot)),
                hanging_of(spelling, tying),
            ),
        }
    }

    // `grace [e5]` — written pitches with no written duration. What a grace
    // takes, and what it takes it from, is a performance profile's choice.
    fn grace_read(read: Reading, spot: NodePath, voiced: Voiced, missing: Text) -> Reading {
        match voiced {
            NoVoices -> refusing(read, spot, missing),
            NoPitches(at_spot) -> refusing(read, at_spot, missing),
            Voices(at_spot, spelling, listed) -> sounding(
                read,
                spot,
                quote at spot {
                    Grace($listed)
                },
                0/1,
                Silent,
                Closed,
            ),
        }
    }

    // `meter (3, 4)` — the meter changes here, and the bars after it say so
    // themselves.
    fn meter_read(read: Reading, spot: NodePath, numbers: Numbers, missing: Text) -> Reading {
        match beats_of(spot, numbers) {
            None -> refusing(read, spot, missing),
            Some(beats) -> sounding(
                read,
                spot,
                quote at spot {
                    MeterChange($beats)
                },
                0/1,
                Silent,
                Closed,
            ),
        }
    }

    // `bar (4, 4) { … }` and `pickup (1, 4) { … }` — a measure of stated
    // length. A pickup is a bar with fewer beats in it, so the package has no
    // pickup and there is nothing here to tell the two apart.
    fn barred(spot: NodePath, numbers: Numbers) -> Result<Form, Text> {
        match numbers {
            TwoNumbers(first, count, second, unit) -> Ok(Barred(
                quote at spot {
                    Beats($first, $second)
                },
                ratio_div(count, nonzero(unit)),
            )),
            _ -> Err("this bar does not say what it measures"),
        }
    }

    // `tuplet (3, 2) { … }` — three in the time of two, and the bracket is
    // kept rather than resolved: what the notes become is `realize`'s answer.
    fn grouped(numbers: Numbers) -> Result<Form, Text> {
        match numbers {
            TwoNumbers(first, played, second, against) -> match ratio_equal(played, 0/1) {
                true -> Err("this tuplet plays no notes in the time of some"),
                false -> Ok(Grouped(first, second, ratio_div(against, nonzero(played)))),
            },
            _ -> Err("this tuplet states no ratio"),
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------- the header

    // The head this reading states, with one more field filled in, or the
    // refusal that says it was stated twice. A second statement of the same
    // word is not a correction: nothing here is written in order, so the
    // reader has no way to say which of the two the composer meant.
    fn stating(
        head: Head,
        field: Says,
        given: Syntax<Expr>,
        node: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Result<Head, Refusal> {
        match field {
            AsInstrument(word) -> match head.instrument {
                Absent -> Ok(head with {
                    instrument = Stated(given)
                }),
                Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
            },
            AsTransposing(word) -> match head.shift {
                Absent -> Ok(head with {
                    shift = Stated(given)
                }),
                Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
            },
            AsClef(word) -> match head.written_clef {
                Absent -> Ok(head with {
                    written_clef = Stated(given)
                }),
                Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
            },
            AsSpelling(word) -> match head.spelled {
                Absent -> Ok(head with {
                    spelled = Stated(given)
                }),
                Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
            },
            AsKey(word) -> match head.tonality {
                Absent -> Ok(head with {
                    tonality = Stated(given)
                }),
                Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
            },
            AsTime(word) -> match head.beats {
                Absent -> Ok(head with {
                    beats = Stated(given)
                }),
                Stated(already) -> Err(Refused(node, "this staff says this twice")),
            },
        }
    }

    // A header field, read and put away. Nothing is placed: a header is what
    // the staff is, not something on it.
    fn heading(
        read: Reading,
        field: Says,
        given: Syntax<Expr>,
        node: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Reading {
        match stating(read.head, field, given, node) {
            Ok(stated) -> read with {
                head = stated
            },
            Err(why) -> read with {
                refusal = first_refusal(read.refusal, why)
            },
        }
    }

    // Why a header field did not take exactly one word. Two words means one of
    // them stands after the field's own argument, and nothing here reads a
    // word in that position — so the sentence names *that* word rather than
    // reporting the field as empty, which it is not.
    fn stray_word(read: Reading, spot: NodePath, words: Words) -> Reading {
        match words {
            TwoWords(first, second) -> refusing(
                read,
                second.spot,
                "this staff does not know this word",
            ),
            _ -> refusing(read, spot, "this staff field says nothing"),
        }
    }

    // `instrument "bb_clarinet"` and `transposing M2` — a literal spliced as
    // the composer wrote it.
    fn literal_header(
        read: Reading,
        spot: NodePath,
        field: Says,
        kind: TokenKind,
        words: Words,
    ) -> Reading {
        match only_word(words) {
            None -> stray_word(read, spot, words),
            Some(said) -> if token_kind_equal(said.kind, kind) {
                given_header(read, field, said)
            } else {
                refusing(read, said.spot, "this staff field was given the wrong kind of word")
            },
        }
    }

    fn given_header(read: Reading, field: Says, said: Said) -> Reading {
        match expression_at(read.region, said.spot) {
            None -> refusing(read, said.spot, "this staff cannot read this"),
            Some(given) -> heading(read, field, given, node_at(read.region, said.spot)),
        }
    }

    // `clef treble` and `spelling shortest_readable` — a word this adapter
    // knows, or the refusal that says it does not.
    fn named_header(
        read: Reading,
        field: Says,
        said: Said,
        found: Option<Syntax<Expr>>,
    ) -> Reading {
        match found {
            None -> refusing(read, said.spot, "this staff does not know this word"),
            Some(given) -> heading(read, field, given, node_at(read.region, said.spot)),
        }
    }

    // `key d major` — the key this staff is written in, looked up rather than
    // built.
    //
    // The one construction site in this adapter that a splice cannot reach. A
    // key literal is `key`, a *pitch-class token*, and a *mode token*, and §2
    // of `../../../docs/rules/language/11-quotation.md` says a splice stands
    // where a whole node stands and never inside one — so `quote at here { key
    // $tonic $mode }` is not a quotation the parser reads, and hand-building
    // the three tokens is what this rewrite deleted. The key names are a table
    // instead, exactly as `clef` and `spelling` are and for the reason §4 of
    // that document gives for notation keywords. It costs an arm per key and
    // buys back a wrong answer: the built version dropped the sharp of `key f#
    // major` on the floor, because a sharp is a token of its own and the
    // builder was handed only the identifier before it.
    fn key_named(here: NodePath, tonic: Text, mode: Text) -> Option<Syntax<Expr>> {
        match text_join([tonic, " ", mode]) {
            "a major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key a major
            }),
            "ab major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key ab major
            }),
            "b major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key b major
            }),
            "bb major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key bb major
            }),
            "c major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key c major
            }),
            "cb major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key cb major
            }),
            "d major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key d major
            }),
            "db major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key db major
            }),
            "e major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key e major
            }),
            "eb major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key eb major
            }),
            "f major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key f major
            }),
            "g major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key g major
            }),
            "gb major" -> Some(quote at here {
                key gb major
            }),
            "a minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key a minor
            }),
            "ab minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key ab minor
            }),
            "b minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key b minor
            }),
            "bb minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key bb minor
            }),
            "c minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key c minor
            }),
            "d minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key d minor
            }),
            "e minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key e minor
            }),
            "eb minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key eb minor
            }),
            "f minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key f minor
            }),
            "g minor" -> Some(quote at here {
                key g minor
            }),
            _ -> None,
        }
    }

    // One header word, with everything held since it was written.
    //
    // `time (4, 4)` is a different word from the `meter (3, 4)` that changes
    // it, and the region grouper is the reason: an adapter cannot see lines,
    // so it cannot tell a word written above the music from the same word
    // written among it. Two words is what that costs.
    fn stated_read(
        read: Reading,
        spot: NodePath,
        field: Says,
        numbers: Numbers,
        words: Words,
    ) -> Reading {
        match field {
            AsInstrument(word) -> literal_header(read, spot, field, TokenKind::String, words),
            AsTransposing(word) -> literal_header(
                read,
                spot,
                field,
                TokenKind::IntervalLiteral,
                words,
            ),
            AsClef(word) -> match only_word(words) {
                None -> stray_word(read, spot, words),
                Some(said) -> named_header(
                    read,
                    field,
                    said,
                    clef_named(said.spot, said.spelling),
                ),
            },
            AsSpelling(word) -> match only_word(words) {
                None -> stray_word(read, spot, words),
                Some(said) -> named_header(
                    read,
                    field,
                    said,
                    spelling_named(said.spot, said.spelling),
                ),
            },
            AsKey(word) -> match words {
                TwoWords(first, second) -> named_header(
                    read,
                    field,
                    first,
                    key_named(first.spot, first.spelling, second.spelling),
                ),
                _ -> refusing(read, spot, "this key names no tonic and no mode"),
            },
            AsTime(word) -> match beats_of(spot, numbers) {
                None -> refusing(read, spot, "this staff does not say what time it opens in"),
                Some(beats) -> heading(read, field, beats, node_at(read.region, spot)),
            },
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------- the flush

    // A sounding word, with whatever it was handed.
    fn sings_read(
        read: Reading,
        spot: NodePath,
        sings: Sounding,
        missing: Text,
        held: Pending,
    ) -> Reading {
        match sings {
            AsRest(word) -> rest_read(read, spot, held.length, missing),
            AsGrace(word) -> grace_read(read, spot, held.voiced, missing),
            AsMeter(word) -> meter_read(read, spot, held.numbers, missing),
        }
    }

    // A nesting word, with the numbers it stated and the body it took.
    fn nests_read(
        read: Reading,
        spot: NodePath,
        form: Nesting,
        missing: Text,
        held: Pending,
    ) -> Reading {
        match held.taken {
            NoBody -> refusing(read, spot, missing),
            Took(body_at, body) -> match formed(spot, form, held.numbers, missing) {
                Err(why) -> refusing(read, spot, why),
                Ok(made) -> nesting(read, spot, made, body),
            },
        }
    }

    // What a nesting word makes of the numbers it stated. Written time counts
    // the page and not the performance, so `repeat (2) { … }` covers its body
    // once: how many times it sounds is a reading of the score.
    fn formed(
        spot: NodePath,
        form: Nesting,
        numbers: Numbers,
        missing: Text,
    ) -> Result<Form, Text> {
        match form {
            AsBar(word) -> barred(spot, numbers),
            AsSlur(word) -> Ok(Slurred),
            AsTuplet(word) -> grouped(numbers),
            AsRepeat(word) -> match numbers {
                OneNumber(first, value) -> Ok(Looped(first)),
                _ -> Err(missing),
            },
            AsEnding(word) -> match numbers {
                OneNumber(first, value) -> Ok(Volta(first)),
                _ -> Err(missing),
            },
        }
    }

    // A word, and what it does with everything held since it was written.
    fn word_read(read: Reading, held: Pending, known: Knows) -> Reading {
        match known {
            Unknown(word) -> refusing(read, word.spot, "this staff does not know this word"),
            Nests(word, form, missing) -> nests_read(read, word.spot, form, missing, held),
            Sings(word, sings, missing) -> sings_read(read, word.spot, sings, missing, held),
            States(word, field) -> stated_read(
                read,
                word.spot,
                field,
                held.numbers,
                held.words,
            ),
        }
    }

    // Anything held and never claimed is something this staff did not
    // understand, and it says so where it was written.
    fn leftover(read: Reading, here: NodePath, held: Pending) -> Reading {
        match held.taken {
            Took(spot, body) -> refusing(
                read,
                spot,
                "this staff does not know what holds this",
            ),
            NoBody -> match held.numbers {
                NoNumbers -> match held.length {
                    NoLength -> read,
                    _ -> refusing(read, here, "this written value belongs to nothing"),
                },
                _ -> refusing(
                    read,
                    here,
                    "this staff does not know what these numbers are for",
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // Everything in hand, finished and placed, so that what arrives next
    // starts from nothing. Nothing having begun it, either a chord's brackets
    // did or it belongs to nothing.
    fn flushed(read: Reading, here: NodePath, held: Pending) -> Reading {
        match held.started {
            Sung(said) -> note_read(read, said.spot, said.spelling, held.length, held.tying),
            Worded(known) -> word_read(read, held, known),
            Nothing -> match held.voiced {
                NoVoices -> leftover(read, here, held),
                NoPitches(spot) -> refusing(read, spot, "this chord holds no pitches"),
                Voices(spot, spelling, listed) -> chord_read(
                    read,
                    spot,
                    spelling,
                    listed,
                    held.length,
                    held.tying,
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    fn cleared(read: Reading, here: NodePath, held: Pending) -> State {
        Body(flushed(read, here, held), nothing_held)
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------ the reader

    // Whether the reader looks at a token at all.
    //
    // Whitespace, the slash between a pitch and its written value, and the
    // comma between two numbers are read nowhere: in a body they change
    // nothing, between parentheses they state nothing, and between brackets
    // they are not a pitch. Most of a page's tokens are the space around its
    // notation, and this says so before one is made into a `Said`. The chain
    // is short on purpose: a case tree's methods for constructors that carry
    // nothing are values and `if` is one such tree, so a level here is paid by
    // every token on the page and a level in `kinded` by only the looked-at
    // ones.
    data Sighted {
        Passed(kind: TokenKind),
        Seen(kind: TokenKind),
    }

    fn sighted(kind: TokenKind) -> Sighted {
        if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::Whitespace) {
            Passed(kind)
        } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::Slash) {
            Passed(kind)
        } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::Comma) {
            Passed(kind)
        } else { Seen(kind) }
    }

    // What a token the reader looked at is, by what the lexer called it.
    //
    // An identifier never arrives here: the traversal hands words over by
    // their own branch, so a word is known to be one before this is read.
    data Kinded {
        Numbering(said: Said),
        Pitching(said: Said),
        Dotting(said: Said),
        Joining(said: Said),
        Passing(said: Said),
        Wording(said: Said),
    }

    fn kinded(said: Said) -> Kinded {
        match said.kind {
            kind -> if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::Integer) {
                Numbering(said)
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::PitchLiteral) {
                Pitching(said)
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::Dot) {
                Dotting(said)
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::Tilde) {
                Joining(said)
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::LineComment) {
                Passing(said)
            } else if token_kind_equal(kind, TokenKind::BlockComment) {
                Passing(said)
            } else { Wording(said) },
        }
    }

    // One more dot on the value in hand. A dot with no divided value in front
    // of it changes nothing; a fourth dot on one is refused.
    fn dot_read(read: Reading, held: Pending, token: Said) -> State {
        match held.length {
            Divided(numeral, dots, value) -> match one_more_dot(dots) {
                None -> Body(
                    refusing(read, token.spot, "this value carries too many dots"),
                    held,
                ),
                Some(more) -> Body(read, held with {
                    length = Divided(numeral, more, value)
                }),
            },
            _ -> Body(read, held),
        }
    }

    // The written value the thing in hand takes, or the refusal that says it
    // was stated twice. A value is spent the moment the note it belongs to is
    // read, so one arriving while another is held belongs to no note that has
    // been read: `c5/4/8` states two and neither is more the note's.
    fn holding_length(read: Reading, held: Pending, spot: NodePath, length: Length) -> State {
        match held.length {
            NoLength -> Body(read, held with {
                length = length
            }),
            _ -> Body(refusing(read, spot, "this note states two written values"), held),
        }
    }

    // `c4/0` is the one number that names no value, and the guard is written
    // rather than caught, for `nonzero`'s reason.
    fn value_read(read: Reading, held: Pending, token: Said) -> State {
        match Body(refusing(read, token.spot, "this is not a written value"), held) {
            unread -> match syntax_at(read.region, token.spot) {
                None -> unread,
                Some(node) -> match syntax_number(node) {
                    None -> unread,
                    Some(value) -> match as_expression(node) {
                        None -> unread,
                        Some(numeral) -> match ratio_equal(value, 0/1) {
                            true -> unread,
                            false -> holding_length(
                                read,
                                held,
                                token.spot,
                                Divided(numeral, 0, value),
                            ),
                        },
                    },
                },
            },
        }
    }

    // A word this staff knows finishes whatever was in hand and starts itself.
    fn opening_word(read: Reading, held: Pending, known: Knows) -> State {
        match said_of(known) {
            word -> Body(
                flushed(read, word.spot, held),
                nothing_held with {
                    started = Worded(known)
                },
            ),
        }
    }

    // A word this staff does not know is an argument for the word in front of
    // it, and a word with nothing in front of it is the word that is refused.
    fn holding_word(read: Reading, held: Pending, known: Knows) -> State {
        match held.started {
            Nothing -> Body(read, held with {
                started = Worded(known)
            }),
            Sung(said) -> opening_word(read, held, known),
            Worded(was) -> Body(
                read,
                held with {
                    words = one_more_word(held.words, said_of(known))
                },
            ),
        }
    }

    // A word, whichever of the four this staff makes of it.
    fn worded(read: Reading, held: Pending, token: Said) -> State {
        match knows(token) {
            known -> match known {
                Unknown(word) -> holding_word(read, held, known),
                Nests(word, form, missing) -> opening_word(read, held, known),
                Sings(word, sings, missing) -> opening_word(read, held, known),
                States(word, field) -> opening_word(read, held, known),
            },
        }
    }

    // One token written in a body.
    fn body_token(read: Reading, held: Pending, said: Said) -> State {
        match kinded(said) {
            Numbering(token) -> value_read(read, held, token),
            Pitching(token) -> Body(
                flushed(read, token.spot, held),
                nothing_held with {
                    started = Sung(token)
                },
            ),
            Dotting(token) -> dot_read(read, held, token),
            Joining(token) -> Body(
                read,
                held with {
                    tying = Tied(node_at(read.region, token.spot))
                },
            ),
            Passing(token) -> Body(read, held),
            Wording(token) -> worded(read, held, token),
        }
    }

    // What a token written between a form's parentheses is.
    //
    // An integer is one of the numbers the form states, and a rational is the
    // exact span `c5(3/8)` writes. Anything else — the comma, whitespace —
    // states nothing.
    data Numbered {
        Counting(said: Said),
        Spanning(said: Said),
        Passing(said: Said),
    }

    fn numbered(said: Said) -> Numbered {
        if token_kind_equal(said.kind, TokenKind::Integer) {
            Counting(said)
        } else if token_kind_equal(said.kind, TokenKind::Rational) {
            Spanning(said)
        } else { Passing(said) }
    }

    // One more of the up-to-two numbers a form states.
    fn one_more_number(numbers: Numbers, numeral: Syntax<Expr>, value: Ratio) -> Numbers {
        match numbers {
            NoNumbers -> OneNumber(numeral, value),
            OneNumber(first, already) -> TwoNumbers(first, already, numeral, value),
            TwoNumbers(first, count, second, unit) -> numbers,
        }
    }

    fn stated_token(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        numbers: Numbers,
        length: Length,
        said: Said,
    ) -> State {
        match numbered(said) {
            Counting(token) -> Parens(stated_number(region, numbers, token.spot), length),
            Spanning(token) -> match length {
                NoLength -> match syntax_number(node_at(region, token.spot)) {
                    None -> Parens(numbers, NoLength),
                    Some(span) -> match expression_at(region, token.spot) {
                        None -> Parens(numbers, NoLength),
                        Some(numeral) -> Parens(numbers, Spanned(numeral, span)),
                    },
                },
                _ -> Parens(numbers, length),
            },
            Passing(token) -> Parens(numbers, length),
        }
    }

    // ------------------------------------------------------------- the groups

    // Everything between one pair of braces, read forwards. The tie the
    // enclosing stretch left open is handed in, so a tie written across a bar
    // line is met inside the next bar rather than carried around it — which is
    // what threading the reader through the recursor's steps buys.
    fn body_reading(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        hangs: Hanging,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> Reading {
        match kids.fold_from_start(
            Body(nothing_read(region) with {
                hangs = hangs
            }, nothing_held),
            fn (earlier, kid) { run_syntax_step(earlier, kid) },
        ) {
            Body(read, held) -> flushed(read, here, held),
            _ -> nothing_read(region),
        }
    }

    // The one word a bracketed child amounts to, where it is a pitch.
    fn sung_said(one: State) -> Option<Said> {
        match one {
            Bracket(said) -> match said {
                Some(found) -> if token_kind_equal(found.kind, TokenKind::PitchLiteral) {
                    said
                } else { None },
                None -> None,
            },
            _ -> None,
        }
    }

    // Every child of a chord's brackets, read as itself.
    fn pitches_of(kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>) -> List<Said> {
        kids.fold_from_end(
            [],
            fn (kid: SyntaxStep<State, State>, later: List<Said>) -> List<Said> {
                sung_said(run_syntax_step(Bracket(None), kid)).fold_from_end(
                    later,
                    fn (said: Said, otherwise: List<Said>) -> List<Said> { Cons(said, later) },
                )
            },
        )
    }

    fn spelled_of(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, sung: List<Said>) -> List<Syntax<Expr>> {
        sung.fold_from_end(
            [],
            fn (said: Said, later: List<Syntax<Expr>>) -> List<Syntax<Expr>> {
                expression_at(region, said.spot).fold_from_end(
                    later,
                    fn (
                        given: Syntax<Expr>,
                        otherwise: List<Syntax<Expr>>,
                    ) -> List<Syntax<Expr>> { Cons(given, later) },
                )
            },
        )
    }

    // The pitches of `[ … ]`, as the list expression the package's `Chord` and
    // `Grace` take, together with the first of them for a tie to meet.
    fn voiced_of(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> Voiced {
        match pitches_of(kids) {
            [] -> NoPitches(here),
            [first, .. others] -> match spelled_of(region, Cons(first, others)) {
                voices -> Voices(here, first.spelling, quote at here {
                    [$..voices]
                }),
            },
        }
    }

    // `{ … }` met in a body: the notation a form holds, or — with no word in
    // front of it — the region's own braces, which `expand` takes back out.
    fn braces_read(
        read: Reading,
        held: Pending,
        here: NodePath,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> State {
        match held with {
            taken = Took(here, body_reading(read.region, here, read.hangs, kids))
        } {
            took -> match took.started {
                Worded(known) -> cleared(read, here, took),
                _ -> Body(read, took),
            },
        }
    }

    // `( … )` met in a body: either the exact span `c5(3/8)` writes, or the
    // numbers a form states. Left to right, because the first numeral is the
    // count and the second is the unit.
    fn parens_read(
        read: Reading,
        held: Pending,
        here: NodePath,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> State {
        match kids.fold_from_start(
            Parens(NoNumbers, NoLength),
            fn (earlier, kid) { run_syntax_step(earlier, kid) },
        ) {
            Parens(stated, exact) -> match exact {
                Spanned(numeral, span) -> holding_length(read, held, here, exact),
                _ -> Body(read, held with {
                    numbers = stated
                }),
            },
            _ -> Body(read, held),
        }
    }

    // `[ … ]` met in a body: a chord's or a grace's pitches. A pitch already
    // in hand is finished first, because brackets never extend a note.
    fn brackets_read(
        read: Reading,
        held: Pending,
        here: NodePath,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> State {
        match brackets_cleared(read, held, here) {
            Body(clear, empty) -> Body(
                clear,
                empty with {
                    voiced = voiced_of(clear.region, here, kids)
                },
            ),
            other -> other,
        }
    }

    // A word in front of brackets owns them — `grace [c5 d5]` is one gesture —
    // unless it already holds something else: `rest/8 [c5 e5]/4` is a rest and
    // then a chord, and `[c5 e5]/2 [d5 f5]/2` is two chords.
    fn brackets_cleared(read: Reading, held: Pending, here: NodePath) -> State {
        match held.voiced {
            NoVoices -> match held.length {
                NoLength -> match held.started {
                    Sung(said) -> cleared(read, here, held),
                    _ -> Body(read, held),
                },
                _ -> cleared(read, here, held),
            },
            _ -> cleared(read, here, held),
        }
    }

    // Which of the three groups a body met.
    data Entered {
        Holding(here: NodePath),
        Stating(here: NodePath),
        Voicing(here: NodePath),
        Ignoring(here: NodePath),
    }

    fn entering(here: NodePath, delimiter: Delimiter) -> Entered {
        if delimiter_equal(delimiter, Delimiter::Braces) {
            Holding(here)
        } else if delimiter_equal(delimiter, Delimiter::Parentheses) {
            Stating(here)
        } else if delimiter_equal(delimiter, Delimiter::Brackets) {
            Voicing(here)
        } else { Ignoring(here) }
    }

    // One group, read under the place it stands in.
    //
    // In a body a group is a form's own punctuation, and which punctuation it
    // is decides what its children mean. Between parentheses there is nothing
    // for a nested group to be but more of the same. Between brackets a group
    // is not a pitch, for the reason the list at the top of this file gives.
    fn group_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
        here: NodePath,
        delimiter: Delimiter,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> State {
        if delimiter_equal(delimiter, Delimiter::Fused) {
            fused_read(region, state, here, kids)
        } else {
            match state {
                Body(read, held) -> match entering(here, delimiter) {
                    Holding(spot) -> braces_read(read, held, spot, kids),
                    Stating(spot) -> parens_read(read, held, spot, kids),
                    Voicing(spot) -> brackets_read(read, held, spot, kids),
                    Ignoring(spot) -> Body(read, held),
                },
                Parens(numbers, length) -> kids.fold_from_start(
                    state,
                    fn (earlier, kid) { run_syntax_step(earlier, kid) },
                ),
                Bracket(said) -> state,
                Fusing(parts) -> state,
            }
        }
    }

    // Everything a fused group spells, read as the one lexeme it is.
    //
    // The interception is in front of the dispatch above rather than inside
    // `entering`, because `entering` is asked only in a body: a chord's
    // brackets hold pitches and a form's parentheses hold a rational, so all
    // three places meet a fused group and all three have to meet it as a
    // lexeme rather than as a group they do not know.
    fn fused_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
        here: NodePath,
        kids: List<SyntaxStep<State, State>>,
    ) -> State {
        match kids.fold_from_start(
            Fusing(
                Parts {
                    kind = TokenKind::Error,
                    spelling = "",
                    opened = None,
                    closed = None,
                },
            ),
            fn (earlier, kid) { run_syntax_step(earlier, kid) },
        ) {
            Fusing(parts) -> lexeme_read(region, state, here, parts),
            _ -> state,
        }
    }

    // What a lexeme is, which its first part decides: a letter opens a pitch, a
    // quality or a size opens an interval, and a numerator opens a rational.
    // Only the first is asked, an accidental and a denominator being parts of
    // something already named.
    fn literal_kind(found: TokenKind) -> TokenKind {
        if token_kind_equal(found, TokenKind::PitchLetter) {
            TokenKind::PitchLiteral
        } else if token_kind_equal(found, TokenKind::RationalNumerator) {
            TokenKind::Rational
        } else { TokenKind::IntervalLiteral }
    }

    // One more part of the lexeme being read.
    //
    // Places rather than tokens, and the first part apart from the rest,
    // because this runs for every part of every literal on the page: what a
    // rational needs of its numerator is where it stands, and what the parts
    // after the first change is the spelling and the end.
    fn part_read(parts: Parts, here: NodePath, kind: TokenKind, spelling: Text) -> State {
        match parts.opened {
            None -> Fusing(
                Parts {
                    kind = literal_kind(kind),
                    spelling = spelling,
                    opened = Some(here),
                    closed = None,
                },
            ),
            Some(first) -> Fusing(
                parts with {
                    spelling = text_join([parts.spelling, spelling]),
                    closed = Some(here),
                },
            ),
        }
    }

    // One lexeme, read under the place it stands in.
    //
    // A lexeme goes where the token it used to be went — `sighted` unasked,
    // none of the three kinds being trivia — with one exception: a rational
    // written where a form is waiting for numbers states them. `time 4/4` is
    // `time (4, 4)` written the way a musician writes it, and the two mean the
    // same because the numerator and the denominator are the same two numbers
    // either way.
    fn lexeme_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
        here: NodePath,
        parts: Parts,
    ) -> State {
        match state {
            Body(read, held) -> body_lexeme(region, read, held, here, parts),
            Parens(numbers, length) -> stated_token(
                region,
                numbers,
                length,
                Said {
                    spot = here,
                    kind = parts.kind,
                    spelling = parts.spelling
                },
            ),
            Bracket(was) -> Bracket(
                Some(Said {
                    spot = here,
                    kind = parts.kind,
                    spelling = parts.spelling
                }),
            ),
            Fusing(inner) -> state,
        }
    }

    fn body_lexeme(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        read: Reading,
        held: Pending,
        here: NodePath,
        parts: Parts,
    ) -> State {
        match held.started {
            Worded(known) -> if token_kind_equal(parts.kind, TokenKind::Rational) {
                fractioned(region, read, held, parts)
            } else {
                body_token(
                    read,
                    held,
                    Said {
                        spot = here,
                        kind = parts.kind,
                        spelling = parts.spelling
                    },
                )
            },
            _ -> body_token(
                read,
                held,
                Said {
                    spot = here,
                    kind = parts.kind,
                    spelling = parts.spelling
                },
            ),
        }
    }

    // The two numbers a rational states. A rational with a part missing states
    // nothing, which no lexer output reaches, and the reading below is the
    // reading `stated_token` performs between a form's parentheses.
    fn fractioned(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        read: Reading,
        held: Pending,
        parts: Parts,
    ) -> State {
        match parts.opened {
            None -> Body(read, held),
            Some(numerator) -> match parts.closed {
                None -> Body(read, held),
                Some(denominator) -> Body(
                    read,
                    held with {
                        numbers = stated_number(
                            region,
                            stated_number(region, held.numbers, numerator),
                            denominator,
                        )
                    },
                ),
            },
        }
    }

    // One more number, read from the place the token that spells it stands at.
    fn stated_number(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, numbers: Numbers, spot: NodePath) -> Numbers {
        match syntax_number(node_at(region, spot)) {
            None -> numbers,
            Some(value) -> match expression_at(region, spot) {
                None -> numbers,
                Some(numeral) -> one_more_number(numbers, numeral, value),
            },
        }
    }

    // One token, read under the place it stands in.
    fn token_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
        here: NodePath,
        kind: TokenKind,
        spelling: Text,
    ) -> State {
        match state {
            Body(read, held) -> match sighted(kind) {
                Passed(passed) -> state,
                Seen(seen) -> body_token(
                    read,
                    held,
                    Said {
                        spot = here,
                        kind = seen,
                        spelling = spelling
                    },
                ),
            },
            Parens(numbers, length) -> match sighted(kind) {
                Passed(passed) -> state,
                Seen(seen) -> stated_token(
                    region,
                    numbers,
                    length,
                    Said {
                        spot = here,
                        kind = seen,
                        spelling = spelling
                    },
                ),
            },
            Bracket(was) -> match sighted(kind) {
                Passed(passed) -> state,
                Seen(seen) -> Bracket(
                    Some(Said {
                        spot = here,
                        kind = seen,
                        spelling = spelling
                    }),
                ),
            },
            // A slash is punctuation between forms and a part of `3/8`, and
            // which it is depends only on where it stands — the one thing
            // `sighted` cannot say. So inside a lexeme nothing is passed over:
            // `sighted` is not asked at all, and every part counts.
            Fusing(parts) -> part_read(parts, here, kind, spelling),
        }
    }

    // One word, read under the place it stands in.
    //
    // The traversal has its own branch for a word, so this staff never asks
    // what kind a word is: it is one already.
    fn word_token(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
        here: NodePath,
        name: Text,
    ) -> State {
        match Said {
            spot = here,
            kind = TokenKind::Identifier,
            spelling = name
        } {
            said -> match state {
                Body(read, held) -> worded(read, held, said),
                Parens(numbers, length) -> state,
                Bracket(was) -> Bracket(Some(said)),
                // No part of a lexeme is a word: the splitter mints seven
                // kinds and `Identifier` is not one of them.
                Fusing(parts) -> state,
            },
        }
    }

    // A node the fixed lexer could not read. It is a fault where notation was
    // expected and nothing at all where numbers or pitches were.
    fn missing_read(region: Syntax<TokenTree>, state: State, here: NodePath) -> State {
        match state {
            Body(read, held) -> Body(refusing(read, here, "this staff cannot read this"), held),
            Parens(numbers, length) -> state,
            Bracket(said) -> state,
            Fusing(parts) -> state,
        }
    }

    // ---------------------------------------------------------- the document

    // A field the staff had to state.
    fn stated_field(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        here: NodePath,
        field: Given,
        missing: Text,
    ) -> Result<Syntax<Expr>, (Syntax<TokenTree>, Text)> {
        match field {
            Absent -> Err((node_at(region, here), missing)),
            Stated(given) -> Ok(given),
        }
    }

    fn document_of(
        here: NodePath,
        instrument: Syntax<Expr>,
        shift: Syntax<Expr>,
        written_clef: Syntax<Expr>,
        tonality: Syntax<Expr>,
        beats: Syntax<Expr>,
        spelled: Syntax<Expr>,
        items: Syntax<Expr>,
    ) -> Syntax<Expr> {
        quote at here {
            Document($instrument, $shift, $written_clef, $tonality, $beats, $spelled, $items)
        }
    }

    // Six things the head has to have said, asked in the order a reader would
    // ask them. Each `?` hands the first refusal straight back out, so what is
    // left on the page is the document being built rather than the six ways it
    // might not be.
    fn documented(
        here: NodePath,
        read: Reading,
    ) -> Result<Syntax<Expr>, (Syntax<TokenTree>, Text)> {
        Ok(document_of(
            here,
            stated_field(
                read.region,
                here,
                read.head.instrument,
                "this staff does not say what instrument it is written for",
            )?,
            stated_field(
                read.region,
                here,
                read.head.shift,
                "this staff does not say how far its written pitch sits from its sounding pitch",
            )?,
            stated_field(
                read.region,
                here,
                read.head.written_clef,
                "this staff does not say what clef it is read in",
            )?,
            stated_field(
                read.region,
                here,
                read.head.tonality,
                "this staff does not say what key it is written in",
            )?,
            stated_field(
                read.region,
                here,
                read.head.beats,
                "this staff does not say what time it opens in",
            )?,
            stated_field(
                read.region,
                here,
                read.head.spelled,
                "this staff does not say how a realized span is spelled",
            )?,
            sequenced(read.region, here, read.items),
        ))
    }

    fn hanging_document(
        here: NodePath,
        read: Reading,
    ) -> Result<Syntax<Expr>, (Syntax<TokenTree>, Text)> {
        match read.hangs {
            Hangs(spelling, node) -> Err((
                node,
                "this staff ties a note to nothing: no note sounds after it"
            )),
            Closed -> documented(here, read),
        }
    }

    // The whole staff, as the one expression a region expands to.
    fn region_read(
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        state: State,
    ) -> Result<Syntax<Expr>, (Syntax<TokenTree>, Text)> {
        match state {
            Body(read, held) -> match held.taken {
                Took(spot, body) -> match body.refusal {
                    Refused(node, why) -> Err((node, why)),
                    NoRefusal -> hanging_document(spot, body),
                },
                NoBody -> Err((region, "a staff region is written between braces")),
            },
            _ -> Err((region, "a staff region is written between braces")),
        }
    }

    // One region of staff notation, read into one ordinary expression.
    //
    // The region's own braces are what the traversal is started outside of, so
    // a region written any other way arrives here with nothing taken and is
    // told what a region is written between.
    let expand = fn (
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
    ) -> Result<Syntax<Expr>, Pair<Syntax<TokenTree>, Text>> {
        region_read(
            region,
            recurse_syntax(
                fn (state, here) { missing_read(region, state, here) },
                fn (state, here, kind, text) { token_read(region, state, here, kind, text) },
                fn (state, here, name) { word_token(region, state, here, name) },
                fn (state, here, delimiter, kids) {
                    group_read(region, state, here, delimiter, kids)
                },
                Body(nothing_read(region), nothing_held),
                region,
            ),
        )
    };
    // ------------------------------------------------------------- writing

    // The other direction, and the one that makes this adapter *generative*.
    // Everything below is the printer's: `expand` names none of it, so none of
    // it is checked in the expansion phase, which is what lets it name the
    // package's own types.

    // A word, then its parentheses if it takes numbers. `bar (4, 4)`,
    // `tuplet (3, 2)` and `time (4, 4)` are one shape and one function.
    fn counted(word: Text, count: Nat, unit: Nat) -> Text {
        text_join([word, " (", nat_literal(count), ", ", nat_literal(unit), ")"])
    }

    fn measured(word: Text, beats: Meter) -> Text {
        match beats {
            Beats(count, unit) -> counted(word, count, unit),
        }
    }

    // Two pieces of notation with a space between them, and no space where
    // either is nothing: an empty body is what `NoItems` writes, and a page
    // ending in a stray space would not be this page reprinted.
    fn spaced(first: Text, second: Text) -> Text {
        if text_equal(first, "") {
            second
        } else if text_equal(second, "") {
            first
        } else { text_join([first, " ", second]) }
    }

    // A form that holds notation: its head, its braces, and whatever follows.
    fn wrapped(
        head: Text,
        body: Result<Text, Text>,
        after: Result<Text, Text>,
    ) -> Result<Text, Text> { Ok(spaced(text_join([head, " { ", body?, " }"]), after?)) }

    // The dots after a written value, one per dot. A fold over the count and
    // not a comparison, because the language has no `nat_equal` and needs
    // none: a count is eliminated by counting through it.
    fn dotted(dots: Nat) -> Text {
        range(dots).fold_from_start(
            "",
            fn (sofar: Text, step_index: Nat) -> Text { text_join([sofar, "."]) },
        )
    }

    // What is written after a pitch: `/4`, `/4.`, or `(3/8)`. The first loss
    // this printer can state — an exact span is a `Ratio` and the grammar has
    // no literal for one below zero, so `ratio_literal` answers nothing and
    // the answer here is a sentence rather than a shorter note.
    fn written_value(held: WrittenDuration) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        match held {
            NoteValue(division, dots) -> Ok(text_join([
                "/",
                nat_literal(division),
                dotted(dots),
            ])),
            ExactSpan(span) -> ratio_literal(span).fold_from_end(
                Err("this staff writes no exact span below zero, and this note holds one"),
                fn (spelt: Text, otherwise: Result<Text, Text>) -> Result<Text, Text> {
                    Ok(text_join(["(", spelt, ")"]))
                },
            ),
        }
    }

    fn tied_on(tied: Tie) -> Text { match tied { Untied -> "", TiedOn -> " ~", } }

    // The pitches of a chord or a grace, in the order they are held.
    fn voiced_pitches(voices: List<Pitch>) -> Text {
        voices.fold_from_start(
            "",
            fn (sofar: Text, sung: Pitch) -> Text { spaced(sofar, pitch_literal(sung)) },
        )
    }

    // What is written at one point of the staff.
    fn written_event(event: StaffEvent) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        match event {
            Note(spelled, held, tied) -> Ok(text_join([
                pitch_literal(spelled),
                written_value(held)?,
                tied_on(tied),
            ])),
            Rest(silent) -> Ok(text_join(["rest", written_value(silent)?])),
            Chord(voices, sustained, joined) -> Ok(text_join([
                "[",
                voiced_pitches(voices),
                "]",
                written_value(sustained)?,
                tied_on(joined),
            ])),
            Grace(ornaments) -> Ok(text_join(["grace [", voiced_pitches(ornaments), "]"])),
            MeterChange(beats) -> Ok(measured("meter", beats)),
        }
    }

    // The written sequence, by the fold the package's own `data` generates. A
    // `pickup` is printed as the `bar` it is: the package has no pickup, so
    // there is nothing here to tell the two apart, and the round-trip law is
    // stated on the value for exactly this reason.
    fn written_items(items: StaffItem) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        staff_item_fold(
            Ok(""),
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                event: StaffEvent,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> { Ok(spaced(written_event(event)?, after?)) },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                beats: Meter,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> { wrapped(measured("bar", beats), body, after) },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> { wrapped("slur", body, after) },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                played: Nat,
                against: Nat,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> { wrapped(counted("tuplet", played, against), body, after) },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                times: Nat,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> {
                wrapped(text_join(["repeat (", nat_literal(times), ")"]), body, after)
            },
            fn (
                anchor: Nat,
                pass: Nat,
                body: Result<Text, Text>,
                after: Result<Text, Text>,
            ) -> Result<Text, Text> {
                wrapped(text_join(["ending (", nat_literal(pass), ")"]), body, after)
            },
            items,
        )
    }

    fn clef_word(written: Clef) -> Text {
        match written {
            Treble -> "treble",
            Bass -> "bass",
            Alto -> "alto",
            Tenor -> "tenor",
        }
    }

    fn spelling_word(chosen: Spelling) -> Text {
        match chosen {
            ShortestReadable -> "shortest_readable",
            ExactValues -> "exact_values",
        }
    }

    // The second loss, and the one the trial note asked for. A transposing
    // instrument is written by naming the interval its part sits above what it
    // sounds, and the written names cover a grid: an interval outside it, and
    // any descending one, has no name a reader would take. The document is not
    // made smaller by leaving it out — the loss says which part could not be
    // written.
    fn transposing_word(shift: Interval) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        interval_literal(shift).fold_from_end(
            Err("this staff has no written name for the interval this part is transposed by"),
            fn (spelt: Text, otherwise: Result<Text, Text>) -> Result<Text, Text> {
                Ok(text_join(["transposing ", spelt]))
            },
        )
    }

    // One command, `replace`: put this text where the node the command names
    // stands.
    //
    // The node is named by anchor and the anchor is echoed straight back, which
    // is the whole of what an adapter may do with a place: the compiler owns
    // the translation to a range. Everything the edit law asks for follows —
    // the patch lands inside the region because an anchor of this region is
    // inside it, nothing outside the named range is touched because nothing
    // else is returned, and re-expanding the patched region agrees with
    // replacing that node because replacing it is what the patch does. Every
    // item this adapter emits carries an anchor, so `replace` reaches any note,
    // bar, tuplet, slur, repeat or ending on the page.
    let edit = fn (
        region: Syntax<TokenTree>,
        command: Text,
        anchor: Nat,
        argument: Text,
    ) -> Result<List<Pair<Nat, Text>>, Text> {
        match command {
            "replace" -> Ok([(anchor, argument)]),
            _ -> Err("`staff` serves one command, `replace`"),
        }
    };

    // A page, written back as the notation it was read from.
    //
    // The header in the order a musician states it, then the notation. What is
    // written here is a region's *contents* — what goes between the braces of
    // `syntax staff { … }` — because the region's name is the importing file's
    // word for this package and not this package's word for itself.
    let print = fn (document: StaffDocument) -> Result<Text, Text> {
        match document {
            Document(instrument, sounding_shift, written_clef, written_key, beats, spelling, items,) -> Ok(text_join(
                [
                    "instrument \"",
                    instrument,
                    "\" ",
                    transposing_word(sounding_shift)?,
                    " clef ",
                    clef_word(written_clef),
                    " ",
                    key_literal(written_key),
                    " ",
                    measured("time", beats),
                    " spelling ",
                    spelling_word(spelling),
                    " ",
                    written_items(items)?,
                ],
            )),
        }
    };
}
